<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alfred Sharpe</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1853">1853</a>–<a href="/years/1935">1935</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alfred Sharpe was a British lawyer, hunter and colonial administrator who became commissioner of the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-africa">Central Africa</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">Protectorate</a> in 1896 and its first governor when it was renamed Nyasaland in 1907, serving until 1910. He extended British control over the territory west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-malawi">Lake Malawi</a> through treaties and military campaigns.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sharpe was born on 19 May 1853 at Lancaster, the son of Edmund Sharpe, a railway engineer whose work took the family to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Educated at Haileybury, he qualified as a solicitor at twenty-three. In 1883 he and a cousin started a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> plantation on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viti-levu">Viti Levu</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiji">Fiji</a>, which failed within a year, and he served briefly as an acting stipendiary magistrate there. In 1887 he went to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shire-highlands">Shire Highlands</a> to hunt elephants and was soon drawn into the war between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-lakes-company">African Lakes Company</a> and Arab slave traders around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karonga">Karonga</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1890 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cecil-rhodes">Cecil Rhodes</a> and the consul <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harry-johnston">Harry Johnston</a> sent him to seek treaties in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/katanga">Katanga</a>. He signed agreements with Mwata Kazembe and Chief Nsama, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/msiri">Msiri</a>, King of Garanganza, sent him away once the missionary Charles Swan revealed what the treaty said. Katanga went instead to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-free-state">Congo Free State</a>, whose expedition shot Msiri on 20 December 1891. Johnston made Sharpe vice-consul in 1891 and deputy commissioner in 1893, and Sharpe's legal training guided the review of European land claims, which issued Certificates of Claim at values of a halfpenny to threepence an acre.</p><p>As acting commissioner he ordered troops against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maseko-ngoni">Maseko Ngoni</a> in October 1896; their leader Gomani was captured and shot, and a heavy fine in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> impoverished his people. Sharpe ruled through district officers, 38 of them by 1905, and minimised the powers of local rulers. In 1902 he doubled the hut tax to six shillings, reduced for Africans who worked 30 days for a European, and later allowed the Witwatersrand Native Labour Association to recruit workers for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transvaal">Transvaal</a> mines. In October 1904, at Ekwendeni, the Northern Ngoni leaders accepted British rule while keeping a measure of self-government. He also promoted the railway from Nsanje to Limbe, opened in 1908.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Sharpe was made a Companion of the Order of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bath">Bath</a> in 1897 and a Knight Commander of the Order of St Michael and St George in 1903. He laid much of the administrative groundwork of colonial Nyasaland, though his efforts to find a profitable cash crop had limited success. After retiring to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> he wrote and lectured on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-africa">Central Africa</a> and travelled widely in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> on 10 December 1935.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alfred Sharpe” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Joseph Thomson and <b>Alfred Sharpe</b> made treaties west of Nyasaland in 1890 and 1891.</blockquote><a href="/empire/northern-rhodesia">Northern Rhodesia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Johnston and his successor <b>Alfred Sharpe</b> used Sikh and African troops to defeat Yao slave-trading chiefs such as Makanjira, the Jumbe State of Nkhotakota and the Maseko Ngoni Kingdom.</blockquote><a href="/state/british-central-africa-protectorate">British Central Africa Protectorate</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Lancaster, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=54.05,-2.8&amp;year=1906" aria-label="Lancaster on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4488 309 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Alfred Sharpe"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4938 579h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4938 579h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=54.05,-2.8&amp;year=1906">What was here in 1906? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
